					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=587467&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608152775" alt="Adam Smascher" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=587467)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;" >Adam Smascher</a></span> <span class="odate time_1332831146 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Mar 2012 06:52</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1403200">
									

<p>To be perfectly honest, I thought it was far more work than it was worth to read enough 19th century ephemeris and technical papers to get a decent feel for the language usage of the time and then write this accordingly. Even if I did my utmost best, someone more versed in the era would still be able to pick it apart. That had me questioning my decision to set this so far back and never really stopped bothering me.</p>
<p>As an in-universe and complete copout explanation, it's possible the Foundation edited this documentation while digitizing it to conform to current standards.</p>
<p>tl;dr Me too, but thanks.</p>

